# Visible-task fidelity checkpoint

Written after viewing ONLY questions[].view, before any author internals, keys,
coverage declarations, or new-figure registrations. Question labels below are
one-based positions in that projection, not yet the author's question IDs.
Blind source solutions were saved first in blind-source-solutions.md.

## Source and subtask coverage visible before keys

- 309 -> q1: both distribution and cube-identity calculations are present.
  The learner selects paired worked calculations rather than producing either.
- 310 -> q2 covers a,b,c,d; q3 covers e,f. Constants and scaled cube bases
  are unchanged. The split distinguishes simple/scaled univariate bases from
  two-variable scaled bases.
- 311 -> q4 covers a,b,c,d,e,f; q5 covers g,h,l; q6 covers i,j,k.
  All twelve exact binomials occur once. Grouping separates elementary,
  rational and terminating-decimal cube bases.
- 312 -> q7 covers a,b,c,d as equality proof certificates. The 1200 claim
  requires an actual integer quotient or an additional evenness argument;
  merely obtaining factor 600 is not enough. q7 offers a numerical quotient
  for d, so it recognizes/checks a certificate rather than constructing it.
- 313 -> q8 covers a,b unchanged, with principal-root direction explicit.
  It asks exact values, not a written account of why the method is most reasonable.
- 314 -> q9 covers a,b,c,d. Original real denominator exclusions are explicit,
  including y=10 and x=-3/2 where cancellation otherwise yields a polynomial.
- 315 -> q10 covers a,b; q11 covers c,d. All original signs, denominators,
  and terms appear. Original real domains are retained.
- 316 -> q12 covers a,d (sum of sixth powers); q13 covers b,c,e,f
  (difference of sixth powers). Partial but valid products are sufficient:
  the source does not demand complete factorization into irreducibles.
- 317 -> q14 covers a,b,c,d as binomial-times-trinomial outputs unchanged.
- 318 -> q15 covers a,b,c and explicitly supplies identity (1), already the
  method specified by the source. Degrees/base powers must still be selected.
- 319 -> q16 covers a,b, including the factor 2 and both original exclusions.
- 320 -> q17 covers both directions, the entire input sets, and closed [1,6].
  The provided general function rule agrees with the actual retained definition
  for these relations (each stipulated input has at least one output).
  It adds the range of I and an explicit x=2 witness to test the distinction.
- 321 -> q18 retains the parabola's opening, vertex, both x-intercepts,
  y-intercept, and closed-interval image. It has NO graph or graphical
  response: producing/drawing the full graph is replaced by selecting textual
  descriptors. It cannot establish plotting, scale, a connected parabola,
  graphical legibility, or independent graph construction.
- 322 -> q19 covers both exact expressions and rational values. Written
  rationality-proof generation is lost; mental exact radical simplification
  remains possible, but the visible response is only the ordered numeric pair.

## Redundancy and leakage observations

No source numbering, internal mapping, answer-key labels, or cross-question
instructions appear in the visible stems. There is no package duplicate of
the same complete task. The repeated cube identity across related tasks is
the source curriculum, not itself answer leakage.

Whole-list alternatives do not individually certify every bundled subtask:
q2 C/D share the first three results; q4 B/C/D share I,II,III,V; q14 A/B/C/D
share III; q18 A/C/D share every graph descriptor and differ only in range.
A learner can often eliminate by a subset of calculations. Under the current
gate, complete-list recognition and reasonable elimination are not an
automatic failure. Record the inability to infer independently produced or
separately mastered answers, rather than inventing such evidence.

q17 has a concrete within-question leakage problem: C explicitly supplies
two distinct outputs at one input for II, immediately yielding the verdict
of B from the function rule in the stem. The input/outcome-direction contrast
is still mathematically present, but C can be used as a supplied answer witness
instead of independently reading the actual polyline. Its exact coordinates
also disclose graph-derived information in a statement to be selected.
This deserves targeted review, not treating every occurrence of evidence
inside an option as automatically disqualifying. A learner must still decide
whether C is actually true; this will be judged after the independent option solve.

No essential algebraic source subtask is silently absent from the projection.
Drawing loss in 321 is real and material, but graph-defining relationships
remain. Rationality/divisibility proof production is not established by
recognizing an exact result or valid certificate. Final verdicts must inspect
the author's concrete limitations without confusing them with math truth.
